// `npm run typecheck` uses this instead of tsconfig.json, for one reason.
//
// tsconfig.json includes `.next/types/**/*.ts` — route-signature validators that NEXT generates
// during a build. That is right for the editor and for `next build`, which regenerates them. It is
// wrong for a standalone type-check, because those files are a SNAPSHOT of the routes that existed
// the last time you built. Change branches, or rebase onto a commit that deleted a route, and
// `tsc --noEmit` reports errors about files that are already gone:
//
//   .next/types/validator.ts(1142,39): error TS2307: Cannot find module
//     '../../app/api/owner/manager-permissions/route.js'
//
// Nothing is wrong with the code; the artifact is stale. That is a confusing five minutes for
// anyone, and `npm run typecheck` exists precisely so the answer can be trusted at a glance.
//
// Nothing is lost by excluding them: `next build` runs its own TypeScript pass over the generated
// validators (and CI runs the build's equivalents via typecheck + lint on a clean checkout, where
// .next does not exist at all). Every hand-written .ts / .tsx / .mts file is still checked here.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "reference", ".next", ".next-*"]
}
